2L88AAAABQntaog","author":{"login":"organicoverlords"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"﻿Fresh live congestion measurement, 2026-08-25 ~15:16-15:22 EEST:\n\n- Local MCP listener is PID 13404 on 127.0.0.1:3000, ~23 MB working set, <9 s accumulated CPU since 11:06. It is not compute-saturated.\n- 12 direct localhost `/health` requests all returned HTTP 200. First/cold request was 115.61 ms; remaining 11 were ~12-17 ms. Average 22.72 ms, max 115.61 ms. This rejects current local-server CPU/RAM saturation as the dominant source of user-visible delay.\n- `.state/transport.jsonl` is now ~55.3 MB. Current telemetry shows a repeating ~15 s health cadence: two short-lived local `/health` connections plus one persistent `via_funnel=true` health request. Each local probe opens/closes TCP and emits multiple JSONL records. This is continuous monitoring/logging amplification even without useful MCP work.\n- Supervisor history proves a separate degradation window: repeated local 5 s health timeouts starting ~13:55 EEST, including 11 repeated failures through ~14:19, plus later local/public failures through ~14:25. It also explicitly records `public MCP health probe failed while local MCP is healthy`, separating local listener stalls from funnel/control-plane failures.\n- Current machine memory is pressured but not exhausted (~2.3 GiB physical free of ~15.3 GiB). An active Unreal workload exists, but the measured localhost response latency above argues against general machine saturation as the present primary bottleneck.\n\nUpdated failure model: treat perceived \"MCP congestion\" as at least three classes: (A) pre-dispatch/non-arrival authorization/control-plane failures already documented here; (B) actual local listener health stalls observable by the supervisor; (C) public/funnel/client-path failures while localhost remains healthy. Do not collapse them into one \"MCP died\" bucket.\n\nNew mitigation hypothesis to test, not yet proven as causal: reduce health-probe cadence, avoid duplicate local probes, suppress routine successful `/health` entries from high-volume transport telemetry or sample them, and rotate/cap `transport.jsonl`. The current probe/log pattern is measured waste, but the 55 MB log itself is NOT_PROVEN as the cause of multi-second stalls.\r\n","createdAt":"2026-08-25T12:26:13Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/organicoverlords/chatgpt-mcp-clean/issues/7#issuecomment-5410347682","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUBs2L88AAAABQqtGjA","author":{"login":"organicoverlords"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Recovery deployed 2026-08-25 EEST.\n\n- Preserved the uncommitted SDK 1.30.0 + supervisor experiment as stash `pre-stability-reset-sdk130-supervisor-20260825`; no user asset was included.\n- Reinstalled the repository-pinned `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.24.3, rebuilt, and replaced only listener PID 6408. ShellMcpKeepAlive supplied PID 22988.\n- Authenticated live smoke PASS: exactly seven tools (`view_image`, `start_process`, `read_output`, `kill_process`, `busy_list`, `busy_claim`, `busy_release`); initialize/list/call, two concurrent jobs, bounded 10k-line output with health responsive, process-tree kill, and cross-session BUSY all passed.\n- Reloaded the committed supervisor without interrupting PID 22988.\n- Quiet-window check: 46 MCP responses, max 123.737 ms, zero early closes, zero aborts; the sole 4xx was the expected stateless GET `/mcp` 405. Local and public health are 200.\n- Corrected the separate agent-routing regression in the canonical shared policy: MCP0 primary when callable, Desktop Commander exact-task fallback, and no Files/Library, saved-memory corpus, issue-history, repo-history, or connector archaeology during route recovery. Synced all ten targets.\n\nThis proves the current server/runtime path. Random ChatGPT-side connector exposure loss remains a distinct upstream condition unless a failing turn produces a re