entify and apply materially changed entries, then continue the inherited task without requiring another `go` or correction. Mixed causal diagnosis remains provisional; the no-retrieval boundary is exact.\", \"state\": \"PROVISIONAL\", \"evidence\": [\"01 Reports/2026-08-27_0110_EEST_refresh-memory-noop_slopwall_incident_report.md\", \"02 Evidence/2026-08-27_0110_EEST_refresh_memory_noop_boundary.txt\", \"03 Fixtures and Experiments/2026-08-27_0110_EEST_refresh_memory_noop_next_action.json\", \"conversation:2026-08-27-current-chat\"], \"supersedes\": [], \"id\": \"mem-20260827-refresh-memory-noop\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-27T01:10:58+03:00\"}, {\"id\": \"mem-20260826-correction-regurgitation-incident\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-26T23:04:25+03:00\", \"kind\": \"correction\", \"scope\": \"response-quality\", \"title\": \"Correction-regurgitation displaces active work\", \"tags\": [\"incident-report\", \"slopwall\", \"correction-integration\", \"response-quality\", \"regression\"], \"text\": \"2026-08-26 response-quality incident: while the active task was to study historical July communication, repeated user corrections about slopwall/filler were converted into meta-responses about how the assistant would respond better. The strongest recurrence occurred immediately after the user said that regurgitating a correction is itself a major failure: the assistant answered 'Noted' and paraphrased that same correction, displacing the historical-analysis task again; it then improvised a noncanonical incident report and required another correction to use the real skill. Evidence-supported causal model: feedback was treated as a new acknowledgement/explanation task instead of a silent update to action selection, creating correction -> meta-acknowledgement/paraphrase -> task displacement -> further correction. Correct counterfactual: integrate the correction without restating it and continue the inherited task, surfacing only new evidence, result, contradiction, or necessary decision. The incident-report skill was subsequently simplified to one evidence-backed analysis whose reusable finding must be written into searchable memory; no second analysis or separate retrieval workflow is required.\", \"state\": \"PROVEN\", \"evidence\": [\"01 Reports/2026-08-26_2236_EEST_correction-regurgitation_incident_report.md\", \"02 Evidence/2026-08-26_2236_EEST_correction_regurgitation_boundary.txt\", \"03 Fixtures and Experiments/2026-08-26_2236_EEST_correction_regurgitation_next_action.json\", \"github:organicoverlords/regression-research#89\"], \"supersedes\": []}, {\"kind\": \"lesson\", \"scope\": \"orchestration\", \"tags\": [\"wip\", \"runners\", \"mcp\", \"hypothesis\"], \"text\": \"Machine-wide oversubscription from independent CI runner/build/validation slots is a proven pressure source, but its causal contribution to MCP/tool-call drift, correction burden, or handle growth is not yet established. Treat that correlation as PROVISIONAL until joined against the five-day event timeline; high handle count alone is not evidence of a leak.\", \"state\": \"PROVISIONAL\", \"evidence\": [\"github:organicoverlords/regression-research#28\", \"github:organicoverlords/chatgpt-mcp-clean#8\"], \"supersedes\": [], \"id\": \"mem-20260825-22e6a820\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-25T15:17:27+03:00\"}, {\"kind\": \"correction\", \"scope\": \"mcp\", \"tags\": [\"incident\", \"slopwall\", \"measurement\", \"non-arrival\", \"regression\"], \"title\": \"Successful MCP arrivals cannot measure non-arrival reliability\", \"text\": \"2026-08-27 slopwall incident: while comparing old/new MCP reliability, the assistant used server response logs and said per-active-session call rate was roughly unchanged. That metric was invalid because the proven pre-dispatch `Connection failed` events often never reach 3003 and are absent from server logs. User screenshots then showed repeated failures during ordinary worker source/audit work, not just synthetic probes. Contributing causes: observability bias toward precise server telemetry, premature quantitative closure, anchoring on the concurrency explanation, and correction-regurgitation instead of completing the regression decision. Required rule: for non-arrival failures, client/worker-visible attempts, explicit failures, rediscoveries, and completions are the reliability denominator; server logs only classify where failed attempts disappeared. Do not declare better/same/worse or block rollback from censored arrival data.\", \"state\": \"PROVEN\", \"evidence\": [\"report:01 Reports/2026-08-27_0108_EEST_mcp-successful-arrivals-denominator-slopwall_incident_report.md\", \"conversation-screenshots:2026-08-27\"], \"supersedes\": [], \"id\": \"mem-20260827-807066be\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-27T01:10:31+03:00\"}]\r\ncanonical memory GitHub sync NOT_PROVEN: memory id conflict: mem-20260827-refresh-memory-noop\r\n[{\"kind\": \"correction\", \"scope\": \"orchestration\", \"tags\": [\"busy\", \"mcp\", \"control-plane\", \"ownership\"], \"title\": \"MCP BUSY is authoritative live ownership\", \"text\": \"2026-08-26 control-plane correction: MCP0 BUSY claim state is the sole live ownership authority for shared mutation. GitHub issue BUSY titles are human-visible projections only and cannot establish ownership. If the matching MCP claim is absent, the GitHub BUSY marker is stale and must not block work. Workers must busy_list then busy_claim exact scope before mutation, and busy_release immediately when mutation stops/switches/handoffs. This supersedes the earlier direction that GitHub BUSY was authoritative.\", \"state\": \"PROVEN\", \"evidence\": [\"git:agents@f49d23624b199a5be97588df1c74d981ced677f3\", \"github:organicoverlords/agents#8,#9\", \"user-correction:2026-08-26\"], \"supersedes\": [\"mem-20260826-f9a5c3d2\"], \"id\": \"mem-20260826-0743b23f\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-26T23:48:22+03:00\"}, {\"kind\": \"lesson\", \"scope\": \"orchestration\", \"tags\": [\"wip\", \"runners\", \"mcp\", \"hypothesis\"], \"text\": \"Machine-wide oversubscription from independent CI runner/build/validation slots is a proven pressure source, but its causal contribution to MCP/tool-call drift, correction burden, or handle growth is not yet established. Treat that correlation as PROVISIONAL until joined against the five-day event timeline; high handle count alone is not evidence of a leak.\", \"state\": \"PROVISIONAL\", \"evidence\": [\"github:organicoverlords/regression-research#28\", \"github:organicoverlords/chatgpt-mcp-clean#8\"], \"supersedes\": [], \"id\": \"mem-20260825-22e6a820\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-25T15:17:27+03:00\"}, {\"id\": \"mem-20260825-mcp-trigger-status\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-25T11:31:10+03:00\", \"kind\": \"fact\", \"scope\": \"mcp\", \"tags\": [\"size\", \"12kb\", \"absolute-path\", \"hypothesis\"], \"text\": \"Payload size around 12 KB and absolute external paths remain unproven as triggers for ChatGPT MCP safety/routing stalls; bounded output and relative paths are mitigations, not established cures.\", \"state\": \"PROVEN\", \"evidence\": [\"github:organicoverlords/chatgpt-mcp-clean#7\"], \"supersedes\": []}, {\"id\": \"mem-20260825-mcp-nonarrival\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-25T11:31:09+03:00\", \"kind\": \"lesson\", \"scope\": \"mcp\", \"tags\": [\"routing\", \"safety\", \"non-arrival\", \"classifier\"], \"text\": \"A ChatGPT MCP failure with zero corresponding server arrival is a different failure class from a locally observed transport/server failure; do not diagnose zero-arrival as local server death or hammer retries.\", \"state\": \"PROVEN\", \"evidence\": [\"github:organicoverlords/chatgpt-mcp-clean#7\"], \"supersedes\": [\"mem-20260825-mcp-6kb-hard\"]}, {\"id\": \"mem-20260825-bank-journal\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-25T11:31:06+03:00\", \"kind\": \"decision\", \"scope\": \"memory\", \"tags\": [\"bank\", \"jsonl\", \"git\", \"shared\"], \"text\": \"Use one compact append-only tagged JSONL journal as shared history for ChatGPT and local/MCP workers; Git provides audit/recovery and rich evidence stays behind pointers.\", \"state\": \"PROVEN\", \"evidence\": [\"github:organicoverlords/regression-research#10\"], \"supersedes\": []}]\r\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04020-d987-7a20-90ff-775c14226e95","create_time":1787782511.2308683}}}
C:\Users\Lauri\.codex\sessions\2026\08\26\rollout-2026-08-26T20-15-38-01a03f11-fbf0-71b3-afb7-52b96a008d3a.jsonl:1201:{"timestamp":"2026-08-26T22:15:16.876Z","ordinal":1200,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a03f11-fbf0-71b3-afb7-52b96a008d3a","turn_id":"01a04020-d987-7a20-90ff-775c14226e95","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-c060f435-4e84-40b6-bdcc-4366493e997f","process_id":"82110","command":["C:\\Program Files\\PowerShell\\7\\pwsh.exe","-Command","Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\\Users\\Lauri\\.codex\\attachments\\f4fd3028-8821-4e4e-aa27-cb4b9d2d2b19\\pasted-text.txt' -Raw"],"cwd":"file:///C:/Users/Lauri/Documents/Codex/2026-08-26/dsa","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\\Users\\Lauri\\.codex\\attachments\\f4fd3028-8821-4e4e-aa27-cb4b9d2d2b19\\pasted-text.txt' -Raw","name":"pasted-text.txt","path":"C:/Users/Lauri/.codex/attachments/f4fd3028-8821-4e4e-aa27-cb4b9d2d2b19/pasted-text.txt"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"Investigate the current ChatGPT → MCP0 connection/regression problem from primary evidence. Diagnose first. Do not fix, restart, redeploy, change OAuth state, modify routing, alter worker policy, or change the MCP server until the causal boundary is actually isolated.\r\n\r\nStart by reading the repo `AGENTS.md` and the relevant existing evidence/logging tools. Preserve all current state.\r\n\r\nCurrent architecture:\r\n\r\nChatGPT connector → `https://kone.tailbf0440.ts.net` → Tailscale Funnel/public path → stable front door `127.0.0.1:3003` → active MCP backend, normally `3002`.\r\n\r\nRelevant historical code states:\r\n\r\n* `cd3be1b` — Aug 25 server that corresponded with a notably quiet operating period.\r\n* `4fc48a6` — PR #14 stable front-door architecture.\r\n* `609dfaf` — front-door request-boundary logging.\r\n* `512e5e5` / merge `c2fa477` — front-door errored-client socket cleanup.\r\n\r\nImportant observations already established:\r\n\r\n* The durable transport log contains well over 100k HTTP requests, so use logs rather than tiny synthetic samples whenever possible.\r\n* A quiet `cd3be1b` window had roughly 577 MCP tool calls over ~8.3 hours, about 70/hour.\r\n* A recent current/front-door window had roughly 2,700 MCP tool calls over ~2.8 hours, around 950–1,000/hour.\r\n* Historical Aug 23–24 periods were even busier, sometimes 1,400–2,100 tool calls/hour.\r\n* Therefore current high traffic is not unprecedented; there was an earlier high-churn regime, then a quiet Aug 25 regime, then traffic increased again on Aug 26.\r\n* Do NOT interpret aggregate calls/hour without controlling for the number of active workers/sessions and task mix.\r\n* `caller_id` usually maps to the `x-openai-session` / MCP session identity. In the recent window almost all tool calls had a corresponding non-null session fingerprint.\r\n* The quiet Aug 25 period often had about one active session per 15-minute bucket, while the current period often had several. This makes a naive 70/hour vs 1,000/hour comparison workload-mismatched.\r\n* Do not conclude either “models are spamming” or “session concurrency is the bug” merely from those aggregate numbers.\r\n\r\nFront-door behavior already checked:\r\n\r\n* Logged front-door request IDs were not duplicated.\r\n* Each front-door request was dispatched at most once.\r\n* There was no evidence that the proxy was multiplying one upstream request into multiple backend tool calls.\r\n* Therefore proxy duplication is currently a poor hypothesis unless new contradictory evidence appears.\r\n\r\nImportant failure class:\r\n\r\nSeveral real ChatGPT MCP calls have returned:\r\n\r\n`Connection failed`\r\n\r\nFor at least some of those failures:\r\n\r\n1. The MCP tool invocation failed in ChatGPT.\r\n2. There was no matching `front_request_start` for the failed invocation.\r\n3. The same MCP process ID remained valid.\r\n4. A subsequent retry reached the front door/backend and succeeded.\r\n\r\nTreat these as pre-dispatch/non-arrival failures. Do not call them backend crashes or process loss.\r\n\r\nThe unresolved boundary is somewhere before the local front door. Candidates include, but are not limited to:\r\n\r\n* ChatGPT connector/request dispatch\r\n* connector account/session/OAuth behavior\r\n* OpenAI-side connection handling\r\n* public HTTPS/Funnel/Tailscale path\r\n* transient machine/network pressure before a request reaches the front door\r\n\r\nDo not pick one without evidence.\r\n\r\nThe main investigation should answer four separate questions.\r\n\r\n1. When did the quiet Aug 25 regime begin, and when did it end?\r\n\r\nBuild a time series at useful granularity, preferably 5- or 15-minute buckets, covering at least Aug 23 through Aug 27.\r\n\r\nTrack:\r\n\r\n* MCP tool calls\r\n* unique active session IDs\r\n* calls per active session\r\n* `start_process`\r\n* `read_output`\r\n* `busy_list`, `busy_claim`, `busy_release`\r\n* `kill_process`\r\n* read/start ratio\r\n* HTTP status failures\r\n* socket/connection errors\r\n* server PID/generation\r\n* deployments/restarts\r\n* worker count if recoverable\r\n* machine pressure if durable telemetry exists\r\n\r\nFind actual step changes rather than selecting convenient windows.\r\n\r\n2. Did individual workers actually become more tool-hungry?\r\n\r\nControl for workload.\r\n\r\nCompare sessions doing similar types of work where possible.\r\n\r\nUseful metrics:\r\n\r\n* calls per active session-minute\r\n* calls per process started\r\n* `read_output` calls per process\r\n* process starts per session-minute\r\n* BUSY calls per task/session\r\n* retries following failures\r\n* repeated identical or near-identical commands\r\n* process starts that immediately replace another still-valid process\r\n* rediscovery/bootstrap calls after connection failures\r\n\r\nDo not average across long idle periods and call the result “per-session behavior.”\r\n\r\nDetermine whether the current increase comes primarily from:\r\n\r\n* more simultaneously active workers\r\n* each worker starting more processes\r\n* more polling\r\n* repeated retries\r\n* connector/tool-context rediscovery\r\n* duplicated work across workers\r\n* some other identifiable behavior\r\n\r\n3. What exactly correlates with the pre-dispatch `Connection failed` incidents?\r\n\r\nFor every recoverable user-visible `Connection failed` event, reconstruct the narrow time window around it.\r\n\r\nCheck:\r\n\r\n* was there a matching `front_request_start`?\r\n* was there a matching backend `request_start`?\r\n* local `/health`\r\n* public/Funnel health if recorded or reconstructable\r\n* front-door client errors such as `ECONNRESET`\r\n* backend socket errors\r\n* listener PID changes\r\n* backend generation switches\r\n* OAuth registration/token events\r\n* machine CPU/RAM/disk/network pressure\r\n* number of simultaneous active MCP sessions\r\n* MCP calls/sec around the incident\r\n* response size of immediately preceding calls\r\n* whether the failed tool was `read_output`, `start_process`, `busy_list`, etc.\r\n* whether retrying the same operation immediately succeeded\r\n* whether the same process ID survived\r\n\r\nSeparate incidents into distinct classes. Do not collapse everything into “MCP instability.”\r\n\r\n4. What changed between the known quiet regime and the return of high churn?\r\n\r\nBuild an exact timeline of code deployments and runtime state.\r\n\r\nPay particular attention to changes around Aug 25–26, including:\r\n\r\n* OAuth retention/recovery changes\r\n* process-receipt changes\r\n* BUSY persistence changes\r\n* supervisor/keepalive changes\r\n* backend/front-door migration\r\n* connector resets/reconnections\r\n* changes in worker orchestration or prompts/policy\r\n* changes in number of workers running\r\n* any change that causes models to rediscover tools or lose execution context\r\n\r\nDo not assume commit timestamp equals deployment timestamp. Use runtime PID/generation evidence where possible.\r\n\r\nVery important analytical constraints:\r\n\r\n* Do not infer causation from temporal correlation alone.\r\n* Do not compare a one-worker quiet period against a six-worker busy period and call the difference model spam.\r\n* Do not declare the server innocent merely because failed requests never reached it; absence at the server only localizes the failure upstream.\r\n* Do not declare the server guilty merely because the problem appeared after server work.\r\n* Do not use synthetic 8-call or 10-call tests as a substitute for historical rate analysis.\r\n* Do not turn one reproduced failure on old code into proof that old and new failure rates are equal.\r\n* Do not treat a successful localhost health check as proof that the entire ChatGPT → public path is healthy.\r\n* Do not infer execution-context loss from a failed `read_output` if the same process ID works afterward.\r\n* Do not invent a new control-plane mechanism or policy to explain the behavior.\r\n\r\nPreferred output:\r\n\r\nGive me a compact causal timeline, not a generic report.\r\n\r\nFor each suspected transition, show:\r\n\r\n`time → runtime/code state → worker/session load → tool-call behavior → observed failure behavior`\r\n\r\nThen give a short ranked list of remaining hypotheses with the specific observation that would falsify each one.\r\n\r\nMost importantly, identify the narrowest next experiment that discriminates between the top remaining hypotheses without changing production behavior.\r\n\r\nThe desired end state is not “MCP has zero errors.” The practical target is to recover the low-churn, productive worker behavior seen during the good Aug 25 period while retaining fixes that solved genuine defects, and to stop transient MCP faults from causing the whole swarm to enter defensive/debugging behavior.\r\n\r\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"Investigate the current ChatGPT → MCP0 connection/regression problem from primary evidence. Diagnose first. Do not fix, re