pplied worker screenshots showing ordinary work repeatedly interrupted by pre-dispatch `Connection failed` events, including read-only source/audit calls. These are not synthetic-probe-only failures. The assistant had therefore used a censored dataset to argue about reliability and then merely explained the mistake instead of continuing the requested regression decision.\r\n\r\n## Evidence classification\r\n- **PROVEN:** pre-dispatch `Connection failed` attempts can leave no server arrival record.\r\n- **PROVEN:** server arrival/response counts therefore cannot measure attempted-call reliability for this failure class.\r\n- **PROVEN:** worker screenshots show repeated failures during normal work, not only synthetic diagnostics.\r\n- **PROVEN:** the assistant presented successful-arrival traffic decomposition before validating that its denominator could observe the target failures.\r\n- **REJECTED:** roughly unchanged successful-arrival rate per active session implies unchanged MCP reliability.\r\n- **NOT_PROVEN:** true old-vs-new attempted-call failure rate until client/worker-visible attempts and failures are compared on equivalent windows.\r\n\r\n## What drove the poor answer\r\nObservability bias toward precise server telemetry; premature quantitative closure once the concurrency explanation looked coherent; anchoring on the latest hypothesis; and correction-regurgitation instead of using the correction to finish the better/worse/rollback decision.\r\n\r\n## Correct next action\r\nCount client/worker-visible MCP attempts, explicit `Connection failed` events, rediscoveries, and successful completions for equivalent old/current windows. Use server logs only to classify where failed attempts disappeared. Then decide better/worse/same and roll back if current is worse. Do not add more live MCP changes first.\r\n\r\n## Durable lesson\r\nFor a failure class defined by non-arrival, server logs are a censored dataset. Validate that the metric can observe the failure class before using it for reliability or rollback decisions.\r\n# Incident: MCP rate-comparison invalid inference ? 2026-08-27 00:10 EEST\r\n\r\nIncident ID: `incident-20260827-mcp-rate-comparison-invalid-inference-001`\r\n\r\n## Inherited objective\r\nDetermine whether the updated MCP is better, worse, or the same as the previously reliable state; if worse, roll back and diagnose the original root cause rather than regressions introduced during diagnosis.\r\n\r\n## What happened\r\nThe assistant reproduced the same *failure class* on a preserved older MCP server, but the test reused current surrounding state and did not measure comparable failure rates. From that bounded reproduction, the assistant incorrectly concluded that current `c2fa477` was not worse and used that conclusion to block rollback. The user then pointed out that workers were reporting drops much more frequently and that the test only proved the test environment could fail, not that the regression rate was unchanged.\r\n\r\nThe assistant compounded the error by repeatedly reporting what was wrong instead of completing the requested regression decision and corrective action.\r\n\r\n## Evidence classification\r\n- **PROVEN:** The old-server experiment demonstrated only that a pre-server failure can reproduce with older server code under current surrounding state.\r\n- **PROVEN:** The experiment did not establish an old-vs-current failure-rate comparison.\r\n- **PROVEN:** The assistant nevertheless stated that current MCP was not worse / effectively the same and used that claim to reject rollback.\r\n- **PROVEN:** The user supplied contemporaneous worker evidence of repeated pre-dispatch failures and explicitly reported that failures/reroutes had become more frequent after the changes.\r\n- **REJECTED:** ?Failure reproduced on old server? implies ?current error rate is not worse.?\r\n- **NOT_PROVEN:** Current failure rate versus the last demonstrably reliable baseline.\r\n- **NOT_PROVEN:** Root cause of the original pre-server failure.\r\n\r\n## First divergence / wrong next action\r\nThe first substantive error was converting a compatibility reproduction into a regression-rate conclusion. The next wrong action was refusing rollback on that invalid conclusion instead of measuring comparable before/after rates.\r\n\r\n## Correct next action\r\nTreat failure *existence* and failure *rate* as separate questions. Reconstruct comparable before/after error rates from durable evidence or controlled equivalent windows. If the updated stack is measurably worse, restore the last demonstrably better baseline first. Then investigate the original failure from that baseline. Do not keep accumulating live control-plane changes while the regression decision is unresolved.\r\n\r\n## Durable lesson\r\nA reproduction answers ?can this failure happen here?? It does not answer ?is this version equally reliable?? Never use reproduction alone to declare a regression absent or to block rollback. When the user asks whether an update is worse, rate/severity comparison is the acceptance test.\r\n\r\n## Artifact links\r\n- Boundary evidence: `02 Evidence/2026-08-27_0010_EEST_mcp_rate_comparison_invalid_inference_boundary.txt`\r\n- Replay fixture: `03 Fixtures and Experiments/2026-08-27-mcp-rate-comparison-invalid-inference.json`\r\n- Searchable memory: `mem-20260827-cecc8a66`\r\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04020-d987-7a20-90ff-775c14226e95","create_time":1787782555.3666458}}}
