eference files** loaded, including the newest Blender animation/mesh lessons, Unreal GUI/ownership rules, TRELLIS finalization/atlas findings, GPU failure rules, and Hunyuan runtime constraints.\\n\\nFor example, the newest files establish that Blender proof renders must demonstrate actual frame-to-frame pose changes fileciteturn0file8L13-L18, glTF mesh connectivity can be badly fragmented despite looking continuous fileciteturn0file15L11-L22, and Unreal GUI work must be visually captured and inspected rather than inferred from process state. fileciteturn0file17L14-L36\\n\\nSend me what you want done with this set.\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"d648018b-ad9a-4680-aa26-5d40319f98ea\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"c907adee-8e99-4952-957e-832b7585cdfe\",\r\n        \"role\": \"tool\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 3,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"Make sure to include fileciteturn0file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation.\\nUse ref_id \\\"turn0file0\\\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 35 file lines.\\n---\\n[L2] name: vendor-finalizers-must-run-before-judging\\n[L3] description: \\\"Never judge a 3D generator on raw decoder output — check what the vendor's own app runs after generation first\\\"\\n[L4] metadata: \\n[L5]   node_type: memory\\n[L6]   type: feedback\\n[L7]   originSessionId: bef7e8c6-36b0-437d-85a9-2492519bc896\\n[L8]   modified: 2026-08-07T02:04:29.202Z\\n---\\n\\n[L11] Before rejecting any 3D generator, find out what the vendor's own application\\n[L12] runs between \\\"pipeline returns a mesh\\\" and \\\"user sees a mesh\\\", and run it.\\n\\n[L14] **Why:** TRELLIS.2 was rejected for a day on catastrophic topology — 2.03M\\n[L15] faces, 51,502 shells, 116,785 boundary edges. `--no-texture` skipped the\\n[L16] geometry finalizer entirely, because trellis.cpp only enters weld / hole-fill /\\n[L17] BVH / narrow-band DC remesh / winding cleanup / component filter / QEM-150k /\\n[L18] unwrap / bake when PBR exists. With stage 6 enabled: 146,076 faces, one shell\\n[L19] at 99.52% of area, zero boundary edges. A different class of object, from one\\n[L20] flag we had been passing wrongly for two days.\\n\\n[L22] **How to apply:**\\n[L23] - Ask the question for every generator, but do not assume the answer\\n[L24]   generalizes. Hunyuan Mini Turbo was re-tested the same way through its native\\n[L25]   FloaterRemover / DegenerateFaceRemover / FaceReducer: the removers deleted\\n[L26]   exactly zero faces, the raw mesh was already single-shell and winding\\n[L27]   consistent, and the only effect was decimation. Robust proportions moved 0.2%.\\n[L28]   Mini Turbo stayed rejected — its hull is reconstructed as a slab and no\\n[L29]   cleaner fixes that. TRELLIS's finalizer was transformative; Hunyuan's is not.\\n[L30] - Make receipts record whether finalization ran, rather than leaving it implied.\\n[L31] - A watertight result that lost the mast is still a rejection. Topology metrics\\n[L32]   improving is not the same as the asset being better.\\n\\n[L34] Related: [[mesh-render-point-splat-not-face-loops]], [[lowvram3d-stack-state]].\\n\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"c544fd2f-5550-4788-a929-813b71ab39ec\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"d648018b-ad9a-4680-aa26-5d40319f98ea\",\r\n        \"role\": \"tool\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 4,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"Make sure to include fileciteturn0file1L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation.\\nUse ref_id \\\"turn0file1\\\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 90 file lines.\\n---\\n[L2] name: verification-vision-luna-spark-command-code\\n[L3] description: \\\"All visual verification uses approved Luna/Spark routes with capacity checks, plus my own eyes as the third check\\\"\\n[L4] metadata: \\n[L5]   node_type: memory\\n[L6]   type: feedback\\n[L7]   originSessionId: bef7e8c6-36b0-437d-85a9-2492519bc896\\n[L8]   modified: 2026-08-13T12:19:39.788Z\\n---\\n\\n[L11] **Every visual verification uses two vision models and me — never my eyes\\n[L12] alone.** Not just mesh renders. Any image I am reading to decide something:\\n[L13] clay renders, textured renders, texture atlases, mattes and masks, segmentation\\n[L14] previews, Unreal viewport screenshots, conditioning images, multiview sheets,\\n[L15] before/after comparisons, plots.\\n\\n[L17] Approved model routes are capacity-gated and must not use an exhausted or\\n[L18] unknown-credit provider:\\n\\n[L20] - Luna: `gpt-5.6-luna` through the Codex harness, or direct `command-code`\\n[L21] - Spark: `meta/muse-spark-1.2-contributor` through direct `command-code`\\n\\n[L23] OpenCode Zen DeepSeek/Nemotron routes are orchestration/text routes, not a\\n[L24] substitute for a vision worker unless the selected model explicitly provides\\n[L25] vision. Check the selected provider's live capacity before every worker; a\\n[L26] credit/payment failure ends that route and must not be retried blindly.\\n\\n[L28] DeepSeek is never an option here — it has no vision.\\n\\n[L30] ```bash\\n[L31] # Luna through command-code: use the effort requested by the current task\\n[L32] command-code --effort high -p \\\"... <workspace-relative image path> ...\\\" -m gpt-5.6-luna\\n[L33] # Spark: NO --effort flag, it errors out\\n[L34] command-code -p \\\"... <workspace-relative image path> ...\\\" -m meta/muse-spark-1.2-contributor\\n```\\n\\n[L37] Three things break these calls, all silently:\\n\\n[L39] 1. **The image must be inside the workspace.** The model reads it with a file\\n[L40]    tool, and a path outside the working directory is refused. The run then exits\\n[L41]    **0** having printed only its opening line. Copy images into the repo (the\\n[L42]    pipeline already does this — `evidence/compare/<subject>/source.png`) or pass\\n[L43]    `--add-dir`. Never point at `Downloads`.\\n[L44] 2. **A preamble-only reply is a failure, not a terse answer.** \\\"I'll inspect\\n[L45]    both images and compare…\\\" with nothing after it means the call died. Confirm\\n[L46]    with `--output-format json` and look for\\n[L47]    `\\\"stopReason\\\": \\\"permission_denied\\\"` in the `run_end` event — the plain text\\n[L48]    output does not show it.\\n[L49] 3. **`--effort` is Luna-only.** Spark answers `Muse Spark 1.2 Contributor has no\\n[L50]    adjustable reasoning effort` and does nothing else.\\n\\n[L52] Use the effort requested by the current task for Luna. If the task requests\\n[L53] MAX, use MAX only on a provider route whose capacity check passed.\\n\\n[L55] Feed the models measurements alongside the image where measurements exist. A\\n[L56] histogram or a texel-density number turns \\\"does this look wrong\\\" into \\\"which of\\n[L57] these causes fits this distribution\\\", which is a question a model can be wrong\\n[L58] about in a detectable way.\\n\\n[L60] **Update 2026-08-13:** the `command-code` weekly quota is shared — when it is\\n[L61] exhausted, *both* Luna and Spark fail with `You've reached your weekly usage\\n[L62] limit` and exit 5, so there is no independent reading at all. **Prefer Luna\\n[L63] through `codex`, at max reasoning**, which bills separately:\\n\\n[L65] ```bash\\n[L66] printf '%s' \\\"$Q\\\" | codex exec -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"max\\\" -i a.png -i b.png -\\n```\\n\\n[L69] `codex exec` defaults to `xhigh`; `max` is what makes it answer \\\"cannot tell\\\"\\n[L70] instead of confirming whatever was asked. Bare `codex -i … prompt` dies with\\n[L71] `stdin is not a terminal` — the prompt must arrive on stdin with a trailing `-`.\\n\\n[L73] **Why:** my single read has been wrong repeatedly and the user has caught it\\n[L74] every time. The shaman staff was called fixed twice off thumbnails. The\\n[L75] octree-448 heron was called a clear improvement when the user's reaction on\\n[L76] sight was that it did not look much better. A paint result was called good when\\n[L77] the atlas underneath was flat-filled. One pair of eyes asserting a visual\\n[L78] verdict, with nothing independent to contradict it, is the failure mode.\\n\\n[L80] **How to apply:** before stating what an image shows — \\\"this is fixed\\\", \\\"this\\n[L81] looks better\\\", \\\"the matte is clean\\\", \\\"the toes are separated\\\" — send it to both\\n[L82] models with a specific question naming the feature in dispute, then report all\\n[L83] three readings including disagreement. If the models split, say so rather than\\n[L84] picking the one that agrees with me. Never paraphrase a model's hedge into\\n[L85] agreement. The sizing precondition in\\n[L86] [[never-judge-geometry-from-thumbnails]] has to be met before the images are\\n[L87] even worth sending. See also [[vendor-finalizers-must-run-before-judging]] and\\n[L88] [[mesh-render-point-splat-not-face-loops]] for two other ways a render has\\n[L89] misled me.\\n\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"ac11bb4a-4397-48e9-a292-12f91d92027a\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"c544fd2f-5550-4788-a929-813b71ab39ec\",\r\n        \"role\": \"tool\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 5,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"Make sure to include fileciteturn0file2L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation.\\nUse ref_id \\\"turn0file2\\\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 67 file lines.\\n---\\n[L2] name: verify-device-and-progress-in-first-minute\\n[L3] description: \\\"Never leave a long job running unattended before confirming, in its first minute, that it is on the expected device and emitting progress\\\"\\n[L4] metadata: \\n[L5]   node_type: memory\\n[L6]   type: feedback\\n[L7]   originSessionId: bef7e8c6-36b0-437d-85a9-2492519bc896\\n[L8]   modified: 2026-08-08T01:43:31.404Z\\n---\\n\\n[L11] Do not launch a long-running job and walk away. Within the **first 60 seconds**,\\n[L12] confirm all three, and kill it immediately if any fails:\\n\\n[L14] 1. **Device.** `nvidia-smi` shows the job holding VRAM and drawing utilisation.\\n[L15]    A GPU job sitting at ~600 MiB and ~11% while 3 CPU cores are pinned is\\n[L16]    running on the CPU and will never finish in a useful time.\\n[L17] 2. **Progress.** Something is being emitted — a stage line, a progress bar,\\n[L18]    growing output. Silence is not \\\"working quietly\\\", it is unverified.\\n[L19] 3. **Sole occupancy.** Nothing else heavy is started alongside it afterwards.\\n\\n[L21] **Why:** on 2026-08-08 the moss titan vendor paint ran **25+ minutes** against a\\n[L22] previous worst of 881 s across 21 runs. A 20-second sample showed +58.6 CPU-s\\n[L23] (292% of one core), **zero** disk I/O, flat RAM, GPU at 11% — the UNet was\\n[L24] executing on the CPU. That was diagnosable in the first minute and instead cost\\n[L25] the whole run. It was made worse by three Blender rig jobs launched *during* it,\\n[L26] the exact contention in [[render-on-cpu-while-gpu-busy]].\\n\\n[L28] **How to apply:** launch it where the output is visible, take the device+CPU\\n[L29] sample right away, and state the verdict before doing anything else. Never start\\n[L30] a second heavy job while one is running. Wrong-guess theories cost more than the\\n[L31] check does — the face-count and 512-conditioning theories were both wrong here,\\n[L32] while the 20-second device sample was decisive.\\n\\n[L34] See also [[lowvram3d-show-every-stage-no-silent-decimation]] and\\n[L35] [[gpu-windows-silent-vram-spill]] for the other half: flat VRAM at 100% util is\\n[L36] healthy, but flat VRAM at ~11% util means the work is not on the card at all.\\n\\n[L38] ## Resource signals cannot tell working from thrashing\\n\\n[L40] On 2026-08-08 a fat-tree run showed GPU 100%, VRAM steady at 5648 MiB, process\\n[L41] alive, CPU time climbing past 960 s — every signal healthy — and had completed\\n[L42] **zero of twelve steps in 26 minutes**. The only evidence that revealed it was\\n[L43] the job's own per-step log going silent, i.e. the ABSENCE of output.\\n\\n[L45] So monitor for absence, not just for success markers. A watcher that greps only\\n[L46] for progress lines is silent during a crash, a hang and a healthy quiet spell\\n[L47] alike. `tools/watch_trellis.sh` reports PROGRESS (with seconds since the last\\n[L48] line), STALL (repeating, escalating, with GPU state attached) and EXIT (with\\n[L49] whether the output file exists).\\n\\n[L51] Two traps that made this harder:\\n\\n[L53] - **The shell's `tee` copy of the log is not the job's log.** A progress bar\\n[L54]   redrawing with carriage returns never ends a line, so it never appears in the\\n[L55]   teed file. Watch the file `trellis_run.py` writes with `--log`, which is\\n[L56]   flushed per line.\\n[L57] - **Elapsed time is a bad anomaly signal.** 40 minutes read as pathological\\n[L58]   until the receipts were checked: the longest *successful* run on this box is\\n[L59]   the moss titan at 2806.5 s (46.8 min). Per-step cadence is the signal; total\\n[L60]   time is not.\\n\\n[L62] **`dwm` competes for VRAM.** The Windows compositor held 1.4 GB of the 6 GB card,\\n[L63] so TRELLIS wanting 5.1 GB spilled 322 MB into system RAM and every step crawled.\\n[L64] Check `Get-Counter \\\"\\\\GPU Process Memory(*)\\\\Dedicated Usage\\\"` — nvidia-smi alone\\n[L65] will not attribute it. Freeing it means closing windows, which is the user's\\n[L66] call. See [[gpu-windows-silent-vram-spill]].\\n\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"54827046-fd29-428f-894b-200b1e24fc61\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"ac11bb4a-4397-48e9-a292-12f91d92027a\",\r\n        \"role\": \"tool\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 6,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"Make sure to include fileciteturn0file3L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation.\\nUse ref_id \\\"turn0file3\\\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 39 file lines.\\n---\\n[L2] name: verify-scheduler-predictio…24 tokens truncated…177414c1f\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"61da82a9-265f-4fd7-9904-b87f1382c480\",\r\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 71,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"I’m separating **current verified findings from older/superseded conclusions**, then I’ll reduce them to the rules that should actually change what we do next—especially rigging/physics, Unreal import, visual QA, and the production queue.\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"b3d4ff21-c880-4613-ba96-6cd61c65b3e4\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"f86a7b2c-19b5-4977-8740-af385abe4a24\",\r\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 72,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"✅ **Yes. There is a lot of genuinely useful material here, but some of it supersedes older conclusions.** The highest-value information is concentrated in the **August 10–14 findings**, and several of those should directly change the current rigging/physics, Unreal, TRELLIS, and queue workflows.\\n\\n### What I would actually carry forward\\n\\n- **Bird rigging/physics: topology comes before weights or physics.** The August 14 macaw work is probably the most useful recent finding for the current bird work. A folded wing that was physically welded into the torso could not be fixed by weight ramps; releasing the wing perimeter dropped bad stretched edges from **15,668 → 43**, torso movement from **0.066 → 0.003**, and tail movement to **zero**. If feathers/wings are reconstructed touching the body, we should detect and release those false joins *before* skinning, Control Rig, Chaos, or secondary-motion tuning. fileciteturn2file14L8-L25\\n\\n- **Do not trust graph-based rigging measurements until connectivity is checked.** The visually continuous macaw GLB actually contained **11,086 connected components**. Graph-distance weight fields cannot propagate across those splits, and edge-stretch tests cannot measure cracks between disconnected patches. That explains why some parameter sweeps appeared to do nothing. The rule should be: inspect connectivity → selective weld/release → segmentation → weights → motion. fileciteturn0file15L11-L29\\n\\n- **Every derived mesh needs a rest-shape fidelity gate.** A badly decimated macaw lost its head shape and tail feathers while deformation metrics still passed, because those metrics compared the damaged mesh against its own damaged rest state. Before accepting a decimated/remeshed/rigged derivative, compare its rest surface against the source independently. fileciteturn2file16L11-L28 This is especially relevant if we produce lighter physics/animation derivatives while keeping the full-quality visual mesh.\\n\\n- **Never transfer anatomical axes between files.** The untouched macaw GLB measured lateral ≈ world `-Y`, while a derived FBX had `Y` behaving roughly dorsal. Something in GLB → rig → symmetrize → FBX altered the basis. Axis detection therefore belongs in each file's preflight rather than being hardcoded per species or asset. fileciteturn1file2L11-L25\\n\\n- **Blender 5.2 has several silent animation-proof traps we should hard-gate.** `export_apply=True` can consume the Armature modifier while still producing an exit-0 GLB; automatic bone heat can return `FINISHED` while weighting **0%** of vertices; the old `Action.fcurves` API is gone; and Blender 5.2 actions require their slot to be bound or they evaluate empty. fileciteturn0file9L14-L39 fileciteturn0file8L13-L25 These are good candidates for mandatory automated validators before anything reaches Unreal.\\n\\n- **The current macaw visual proof is useful, but not as strong as some status labels imply.** The anatomical basis, left/right sign, and segmentation were independently passed by **Luna only**. Spark was unavailable, so those findings must remain explicitly **one-witness proof**, not two-model verification. fileciteturn1file3L11-L25 That does not invalidate the work; it prevents us from overstating certainty.\\n\\n- **Unreal import should use measured transformations rather than compensating actors after the fact.** The Asylum Demon source GLB and rig were actually upright, textured, and correctly handed; the 90° error entered downstream in Unreal. A clean FBX import reproduced the intended orientation at identity transform. fileciteturn0file6L15-L38 Also, Blender FBX already arrives in centimetres: importing the Fennec at `100` instead of `1` produced a **197-metre animal**. Post-import bounds validation should therefore be mandatory. fileciteturn0file12L11-L29\\n\\n- **Visual QA has a much better verified procedure now.** Generation finishes → immediately render **native appearance + clay** → show the render before analysis → inspect fine details with large crops → use Blender for surface/material claims. TRELLIS already contains native baked appearance; Mini Turbo shape output does not. fileciteturn2file0L11-L25 Fine geometry cannot be judged from 250–400 px contact-sheet figures; disputed features need high-resolution crops. fileciteturn2file6L11-L29\\n\\n- **The renderer itself must be cross-checked before blaming an asset.** A nearest-neighbour software sampler invented severe plate-like texture defects; two vision models and human inspection all agreed the asset was bad because they were all looking at the same incorrect render. Blender showed the actual asset was fine. Therefore, a defect appearing in only one renderer is **NOT_PROVEN as an asset defect**. fileciteturn3file1L11-L44 Point splatting likewise cannot support claims about holes or surface quality; Blender headless is the authoritative surface path. fileciteturn2file2L13-L38\\n\\n- **TRELLIS quality should be judged after its real vendor finalization path.** `--no-texture` had accidentally skipped weld, hole fill, BVH, narrow-band DC remesh, winding cleanup, component filtering, QEM, unwrap and bake. The same raw result went from **2.03M faces / 51,502 shells / 116,785 boundary edges** to **146k faces / one 99.52%-area shell / zero boundary edges** once the intended finalizer ran. fileciteturn0file0L11-L30 Finalization should therefore be an explicit receipt field, not an assumption.\\n\\n- **Do not throw away TRELLIS PLYs.** This is particularly important for disk-cleanup agents. The PLY is the **undecimated master**, not scratch output: the Asylum Demon example was **6.27M faces in PLY versus 435k in GLB**, with vertex colour retained. fileciteturn2file10L17-L38 Disk cleanup should never touch these masters.\\n\\n- **The atlas problem has a verified production fix.** The old 1024 atlas left roughly two texels per triangle across many assets. Production was changed to **4096**, measured at 65.9 texels/triangle in the reported case, with only ~46 seconds additional runtime; the first three resulting assets were visually accepted. fileciteturn0file7L41-L56 So there is no reason to reopen the old generic “misprojection/camouflage” diagnosis without evidence.\\n\\n- **Do not silently decimate generator experiments.** Octree 384/448/512 results at 1.20M/1.65M/2.13M triangles were all previously crushed to 400k, which could erase precisely the quality difference being tested. Raw generator results must remain part of every comparison. fileciteturn1file12L11-L28\\n\\n- **Core ComfyUI Hunyuan3D is now PROVEN and the old “ComfyUI unusable” state is superseded.** On August 10, core ComfyUI—with **all custom nodes disabled**—generated a valid **94 MB, 2.62M-face GLB** on the GTX 1660 SUPER, peaking at **4.58 GB VRAM**. 3D-Pack is unnecessary for this path. fileciteturn2file4L11-L30 The old stack-state entry itself explicitly marks the prior ComfyUI conclusion as superseded. fileciteturn1file13L14-L20\\n\\n- **Do not reopen MV-Adapter I2MV just because its NaN bug was solved.** Its numerical problems were fixed, but the clean 384×20 production run then **lost the actual subject identity**—tower dishes disappeared, the mast collapsed, colour shifted, and geometry floated. That identity failure, not NaNs, is why the route was abandoned. fileciteturn2file3L11-L33\\n\\n- **There are two separate numerical traps worth keeping as reusable guards.** On this specific GTX 1660 SUPER / torch / cuDNN stack, FP16 cuDNN convolution can silently generate NaNs, and the failure is shape-dependent enough that a passing small probe does not clear a 384px run. fileciteturn1file7L13-L24 Separately, changing an SD2.x base checkpoint can silently switch scheduler `prediction_type`; tensors remain finite while the latent diverges until the final black image. Scheduler configuration therefore needs an explicit assertion after any substitution. fileciteturn3file0L11-L34\\n\\n- **The August 12 GPU finding changes how we interpret TRELLIS CUDA crashes.** Repeated `misaligned address` / `illegal memory access` failures were reproduced **5/5 with the card's factory overclock active**, while returning the GPU to stock clocks produced successful runs on the same subject/binary/seed. fileciteturn0file5L11-L35 Therefore the older blanket interpretation “misaligned address = OOM” is no longer safe. The error string by itself does **not** identify the cause. For TRELLIS, check Afterburner/clock state first; for memory pressure, use actual residency/paging evidence.\\n\\n- **Windows VRAM spill is still independently verified.** Going beyond resident VRAM may silently page through system RAM rather than raise OOM, producing huge step-time jitter. The measured Hunyuan case went from wildly varying **74–189 s/step resident** to roughly **18–30 s/step with offload**. fileciteturn0file16L11-L24 So GPU stability and VRAM residency are two separate failure classes.\\n\\n- **Production queue control should remain extremely conservative.** Editing a running Bash supervisor can either corrupt execution or, more commonly, simply leave the old parsed function running while the file on disk shows the new value. fileciteturn2file5L11-L33 Restarting it can orphan `trellis-cli`; one orphan held roughly **7 GB of commit for 20 minutes** and doubled another asset's runtime. fileciteturn2file13L16-L38 Use marker files for runtime control and verify process count after an actual restart.\\n\\n### Conclusions I would now retire\\n\\nThe old statements **“ComfyUI is unusable,” “every misaligned-address error means OOM,” “the macaw has two independent vision witnesses,” “a point-splat can prove holes,” “fine detail can be judged from the contact sheet,” “a good deformation smoke proves the derived mesh itself is good,” and “a visually continuous imported mesh has a usable graph”** should no longer be allowed into agent reasoning as facts.\\n\\nThere is also an important distinction in the August 14 queue handoff: it explicitly says it is a **consolidation of prior findings that was not re-verified during that session**. fileciteturn2file12L11-L16 It is useful as a diagnostic map, but current code should be checked before turning any of those queue details into a new `PROVEN` claim.\\n\\n### What this changes for the current rigging/physics direction\\n\\nThe strongest architecture emerging from all of this is:\\n\\n**source master → connectivity/topology audit → anatomical segmentation → release false welded joints → per-file axis solve → preserve/reference rest shape → rig + verified weighting → explicit animation/export validation → Unreal import with measured scale/orientation → then secondary feather/wing physics.**\\n\\nThat is substantially safer than trying to make Chaos/Control Rig compensate for bad source topology or bad skinning. For the birds in particular, I would make **wing/body release topology, feather-region segmentation, rest-shape fidelity, and actual frame-difference proof mandatory gates before we spend time tuning physics.** memcite\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"8db55ad1-6306-4dd1-ba12-f7cbd80f9b03\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"06d13ed5-15ec-4166-a106-bd642fdb255d\",\r\n        \"role\": \"tool\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 73,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"Make sure to include fileciteturn3file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation.\\nUse ref_id \\\"turn3file0\\\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 39 file lines.\\n---\\n[L2] name: verify-scheduler-prediction-type-on-substitution\\n[L3] description: Substituting a base checkpoint silently swaps scheduler prediction_type; a wrong one diverges instead of crashing\\n[L4] metadata: \\n[L5]   node_type: memory\\n[L6]   type: feedback\\n[L7]   originSessionId: f4758620-4770-4454-949b-772073fabcb7\\n[L8]   modified: 2026-08-10T11:35:41.583Z\\n---\\n\\n[L11] When swapping the base checkpoint a diffusion model loads from, checking that the\\n[L12] *weights* transfer is not enough. `from_pretrained(..., subfolder=\\\"scheduler\\\")`\\n[L13] also pulls `prediction_type`, `beta_schedule` and `steps_offset` from the\\n[L14] substituted repo. Weight substitution is validated loudly by strict\\n[L15] `load_state_dict`; scheduler substitution is validated by nothing.\\n\\n[L17] **Why:** a wrong `prediction_type` does not raise. Every tensor stays finite and\\n[L18] the model output stays perfectly healthy, while the latent walks off the manifold\\n[L19] by a constant factor per step. It surfaces ~25 minutes later as a black PNG, which\\n[L20] looks like a GPU/fp16 fault and is not one. This cost most of a day on\\n[L21] MVGenMaster: a v-prediction model driven by an epsilon update rule, diagnosed\\n[L22] first as a TU116 fp16 defect (wrong), then a VAE fp16 overflow (wrong).\\n\\n[L24] **How to apply:**\\n\\n[L26] - After substituting a base model, print and assert the scheduler config before\\n[L27]   generating: `prediction_type`, `rescale_betas_zero_snr`, `beta_schedule`.\\n[L28] - `rescale_betas_zero_snr: true` **requires** `v_prediction`. At terminal SNR the\\n[L29]   epsilon parameterization divides by an alpha forced to zero.\\n[L30] - SD2.1-768 is `v_prediction`; SD2.1-**base** is `epsilon`. They are not\\n[L31]   interchangeable at the scheduler even though their VAE and text encoder are.\\n[L32] - Diagnose divergence by trend, not by threshold. A latent that is finite but\\n[L33]   grows monotonically at a fixed ratio is already dead; gate on\\n[L34]   `std > ~8` after a few steps instead of waiting for NaN or the fp16 ceiling.\\n\\n[L36] Related: [[sd21-gone-from-hf-local-copy-only]],\\n[L37] [[lowvram3d-fp16-cudnn-convolution-defect]] (a *separate*, still-valid finding —\\n[L38] this one does not retract it).\\n\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"ff0228ab-0c61-40f5-ad29-3146718e5254\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"8db55ad1-6306-4dd1-ba12-f7cbd80f9b03\",\r\n        \"role\": \"tool\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 74,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"Make sure to include fileciteturn3file1L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation.\\nUse ref_id \\\"turn3file1\\\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 48 file lines.\\n---\\n[L2] name: verify-the-renderer-before-diagnosing-the-asset\\n[L3] description: \\\"Before diagnosing a texture or surface defect, render the same asset through a second renderer — the sampler can invent the defect\\\"\\n[L4] metadata:\\n[L5]   node_type: memory\\n[L6]   type: feedback\\n[L7]   originSessionId: bef7e8c6-36b0-437d-85a9-2492519bc896\\n[L8]   modified: 2026-08-07T20:33:11.468Z\\n---\\n\\n[L11] **A defect seen in exactly one renderer is a renderer bug until a second\\n[L12] renderer shows it too.** Render through Blender before writing down any\\n[L13] conclusion about a texture, surface or shading defect.\\n\\n[L15] `workers/render_textured_views.py` sampled textures with a **nearest-neighbour**\\n[L16] lookup and no filtering. A face covering one or two texels took a single texel's\\n[L17] colour across its whole area, and neighbouring faces landing in different texels\\n[L18] met at a hard edge — so every low-density texture rendered as a mosaic of\\n[L19] hard-edged plates. The texture was fine. (Fixed: it samples bilinearly now.)\\n\\n[L21] What that one artifact produced before anyone checked:\\n\\n[L23] - Two fennec paints declared **unusable** — by me and by *both* vision models.\\n[L24] - A \\\"multiview hallucination\\\" root-cause diagnosis from Luna at high effort,\\n[L25]   ruling out texel density, baked lighting and gamma by name.\\n[L26] - A rebake at 4x atlas density to chase it (2048 vs 1024: **mean absolute\\n[L27]   difference 0.45/255**, 1.54% of pixels differing by more than 8 — no visible\\n[L28]   change at all).\\n[L29] - A whole runbook section on UV fragmentation written to explain the plates.\\n[L30] - Roughly an evening.\\n\\n[L32] **Why:** [[verification-vision-luna-spark-command-code]] requires two models plus\\n[L33] my own eyes, and all three agreed — because all three were shown the same bad\\n[L34] picture. Model agreement cannot detect a defect in the image itself; it only\\n[L35] detects disagreement about what the image shows.\\n[L36] [[inspect-image-before-showing-or-dispatching]] covers empty frames and bad\\n[L37] crops; it did not cover the renderer's own fidelity, and that is the gap.\\n\\n[L39] **How to apply:** before diagnosing any appearance defect, re-render the same\\n[L40] GLB through a second, independent path — `workers/render_asset_views.py\\n[L41] --native` (Blender/EEVEE, seconds) against the software rasteriser. If the\\n[L42] defect appears in only one, fix the renderer, not the asset. Ask specifically\\n[L43] whether the sampler filters, whether the shading model is comparable, and\\n[L44] whether the resolution is adequate — the pipeline's own renderer is evidence\\n[L45] about the pipeline's renderer until corroborated. See\\n[L46] [[mesh-render-point-splat-not-face-loops]] for the same lesson learned about the\\n[L47] point splat, which rendered solid surfaces as perforated.\\n\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"850dda11-6e35-492b-91a3-30ff09fdc376\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"ff0228ab-0c61-40f5-ad29-3146718e5254\",\r\n        \"role\": \"tool\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 75,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"61da82a9-265f-4fd7-9904-b87f1382c480\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"850dda11-6e35-492b-91a3-30ff09fdc376\",\r\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 76,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"6eb64b01-2984-40b0-8720-759acc1818af\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"4ee39a5c-a942-41fc-8cf7-402177414c1f\",\r\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 77,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"message_id\": \"f86a7b2c-19b5-4977-8740-af385abe4a24\",\r\n        \"parent_id\": \"6eb64b01-2984-40b0-8720-759acc1818af\",\r\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n        \"ordinal\": 78,\r\n        \"content_text\": \"\"\r\n      }\r\n    ]\r\n  },\r\n  \"marker\": \"[ContextRelay source=ContextRelay agent_id=acceptance-agent task_id=real-acceptance request_id=req_a9224ece0236404987feb97d02fa1fd3]\\nMachine relay: answer this request as the next assistant turn.\",\r\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-08-14T14:57:22.281599+00:00\",\r\n  \"completed_at\": \"2026-08-14T15:00:22.353816+00:00\"\r\n}\r\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a000af-e521-7f52-af60-d5da32ff2eb1"}}}
