url -sS -X POST 'http://127.0.0.1:4096/session/<SESSION_ID>/message' \\\n      -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"parts\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Reply with exactly: API_OK\"}]}'\n\n实际结果：\n\n  * HTTP `200`\n  * 返回 JSON 中确实有 assistant message\n  * 但 `parts` 为空\n  * `info.error.name = \"MessageAbortedError\"`\n  * `info.error.data.message = \"Aborted\"`\n\n返回结构大致如下：\n    \n    {\n      \"info\": {\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\n        \"error\": {\n          \"name\": \"MessageAbortedError\",\n          \"data\": { \"message\": \"Aborted\" }\n        }\n      },\n      \"parts\": []\n    }\n\n### 2. `POST /session/{id}/prompt_async`\n    \n    curl -sS -X POST 'http://127.0.0.1:4096/session/<SESSION_ID>/prompt_async' \\\n      -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"parts\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Reply with exactly: API_OK\"}]}'\n\n实际结果：\n\n  * HTTP `204`\n  * 之后再调用 `GET /session/{id}/message`，会看到同样的问题：\n    * assistant message 存在\n    * `parts` 为空\n    * `info.error.name = \"MessageAbortedError\"`\n\n### 3. `POST /session/{id}/command`\n\n根据本地安装的 SDK 类型定义，`SessionCommandData.body.agent` 看起来是可选的。\n\n但实际行为是：\n\n  * 不传 `agent` 时，服务端直接报 schema 错误：\n    * `Missing key at [\"agent\"]`\n  * 传了 `agent` 之后，请求仍然失败：\n    * HTTP `503`\n    * 响应体为空\n\n所以这里看起来存在 spec / runtime 不一致。\n\n### 4. `POST /session/{id}/shell`\n\n  * 不传 `agent` 时，会有 payload 校验错误\n  * 传了 `agent` 后，服务端返回：\n    * HTTP `500`\n    * `UnknownError`\n  * server log 中能看到 `SessionPrompt.shellImpl`\n\n### 5. `GET /session/{id}/share`\n\n按照 SDK / spec，预期应该是 JSON session/share 响应。\n\n但实际结果是：\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[FEATURE]: prompt_async: return pre-allocated assistant message ID in 202 response · Issue #22925 · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/22925)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last week;     POST /session/:id/prompt_async { \"tracked\": true, ... }  →  202 { \"assistantMessageID\": \"msg_...\" ... We run OpenCode as a backend service (via `opencode serve`) and integrate it with our application over HTTP.\n\n# [FEATURE]: prompt_async: return pre-allocated assistant message ID in 202 response #22925\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nOpen\n\nOpen\n\n[FEATURE]: prompt_async: return pre-allocated assistant message ID in 202 response#22925\n\nCopy link\n\nAssignees\n\n## Description\n\norgito\n\nopened on Apr 16, 2026\n\nIssue body actions\n\n### Feature hasn't been suggested before.\n\n  * [Input] I have verified this feature I'm about to request hasn't been suggested before.\n\n### Describe the enhancement you want to request\n\n## Problem\n\n`POST /session/:id/prompt_async` returns `204 No Content` with no body. Callers have no way to correlate the HTTP request with the assistant message that will eventually be created.\n\nThis makes the endpoint unsuitable for server-to-server integrations where the caller needs to know which message to poll for. The only options today are:\n\n  1. Blind-poll `listMessages` and hope the newest assistant message is the one you triggered — breaks under concurrent requests.\n  2. Supply your own `messageID` in the request body (supported but undocumented as a correlation mechanism) — this only identifies the user message, not the assistant reply.\n  3. Subscribe to the SSE `/event` stream — requires a persistent connection, fragile for backend callers.\n\nNone of these provide a clean correlation between \"I sent this prompt\" and \"this is the assistant's response.\"\n\n## Proposed Change\n\nPre-allocate the assistant message ID before detaching the async work, and return it so the caller can poll `GET /session/:id/message/:assistantMessageID` until it exists and has parts.\n\n### Why this is feasible\n\nLooking at the current code:\n\n  * `createUserMessage()` runs before the LLM loop (prompt.ts#L1283)\n  * The assistant message ID is generated via `MessageID.ascending()` inside `runLoop()` (prompt.ts#L1412)\n  * `MessageID.ascending()` is a pure ID generator (timestamp + counter + random suffix) with no side effects — it can be called earlier and threaded through\n\n### Possible Solutions\n\nSince `prompt_async` currently has a `204 No Content` contract that existing clients may depend on, there are two backward-compatible approaches:\n\nOption A: New endpoint\n\nAdd a dedicated `POST /session/:id/prompt_tracked` endpoint that returns `202 Accepted` with the pre-allocated assistant message ID:\n    \n    {\n      \"assistantMessageID\": \"msg_...\"\n    }\n\n`prompt_async` stays as-is — no breaking change. Clean separation between fire-and-forget and trackable async.\n\nOption B: Opt-in on existing endpoint\n\nKeep `prompt_async`, but if the caller sends `\"tracked\": true` (or similar) in the request body, return `202 + JSON` instead of `204`. Without the flag, behavior is unchanged.\n    \n    // Without tracked flag — existing behavior\n    POST /session/:id/prompt_async  →  204 No Content\n    \n    // With tracked flag — new behavior\n    POST /session/:id/prompt_async { \"tracked\": true, ... }  →  202 { \"assistantMessageID\": \"msg_...\" }\n\nBackward compatible, but overloads the endpoint semantics.\n\nEither way, the server-side change is the same: pre-allocate the assistant message ID and thread it through to `runLoop()`:\n    \n    const assistantMessageID = MessageID.ascending()\n    void AppRuntime.runPromise(\n      SessionPrompt.Service.use((svc) =>\n        svc.prompt({ ...body, sessionID, assistantMessageID })\n      )\n    ).catch(...)\n    return c.json({ assistantMessageID }, 202)\n\n## Use Case\n\nWe run OpenCode as a backend service (via `opencode serve`) and integrate it with our application over HTTP. When a user sends a message, we need to:\n\n  1. Fire the prompt asynchronously (don't block for minutes)\n  2. Know which assistant message to watch for\n  3. Save the assistant response to our database when it appears\n\nToday we work around this with a triple-path reconciliation system (sync call with 45-min timeout + webhook fallback + read-repair on page load), which is complex and fragile. A pre-allocated assistant message ID would let us simplify to: async send → poll for result.\n\nReactions are currently unavailable\n\n## Metadata\n\n## Metadata\n\n### Assignees\n\n  * jlongster\n\n### Labels\n\nNo labels\n\nNo labels\n\n### Type\n\nNo type\n\n### Projects\n\nNo projects\n\n### Milestone\n\nNo milestone\n\n### Relationships\n\nNone yet\n\n### Development--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nopencode/specs/v2/session.md at dev · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/dev/specs/v2/session.md)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; anomalyco / opencode Public ...       -> reusing one message ID for another Session, prompt, or delivery mode fails ... Tool settlement events carry the owning assistant message ID because provider-local call IDs may repeat across turns.\n\nanomalyco / opencode Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 24.3k\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 3.7k\n  * Pull requests 1.1k\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 2\n  * Insights\n\nAdditional navigation options\n\n## Files Expand file tree\n\ndev\n\n## Breadcrumbs\n\n  1. opencode\n  2. /specs\n  3. /v2\n\n/\n\n# session.md\n\nCopy path\n\n[Input: Go to file]\n\nBlame More file actions\n\nBlame More file actions\n\n## Latest commit\n\n \n\n## History\n\nHistory\n\ndev\n\n## Breadcrumbs\n\n  1. opencode\n  2. /specs\n  3. /v2\n\n/\n\n# session.md\n\nCopy path\n\nTop\n\n## File metadata and controls\n\n  * Preview\n\n  * Code\n\n  * Blame\n\n231 lines (168 loc) · 28.3 KB\n\nRaw\n\nCopy raw file\n\nDownload raw file\n\nOutline\n\nEdit and raw actions\n\n# Session API\n\n## Current V2 Core Slice\n\nThe Effect-native core facade treats prompt recording and execution as separate responsibilities:\n    \n    `sessions.create({ id?, location, ... })\n      -> omitted ID generates one internal Session ID\n      -> supplied ID creates the Session when absent\n      -> reused ID returns the existing Session identity\n    \n    sessions.prompt({ id?, sessionID, prompt, delivery?, resume? })\n      -> omitted ID generates one internal message ID\n      -> supplied ID inserts one durable Session inbox row when absent\n      -> exact reuse returns the same admission receipt\n      -> reusing one message ID for another Session, prompt, or delivery mode fails\n      -> exact retry schedules another wake unless resume is false\n      -> resume omitted or true schedules execution after admission\n      -> resume false admits only\n    \n    sessions.interrupt(sessionID)\n      -> interrupts active execution on this process\n      -> waits for runner cleanup and settlement\n      -> clears a coalesced follow-up wake already registered with this coordinator\n      -> preserves durable inbox rows for a later wake or resume\n      -> idle or missing Session is a no-op\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nprompt_async returns 204 after startup failure, leaving empty child sessions that appear frozen · Issue #33394 · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/33394)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; The immediate trigger I observed was the known `session_message.seq` insertion bug, but the user-visible failure is broader: `POST /session/{id}/prompt_async` can fail during startup, publish/log `session.error`, still return HTTP 204, and leave a valid child session with zero user/assistant messages. ... The durable fix should be in OpenCode core/API so plugin/tooling authors do not each need to implement their own bootstrap-detection workaround. ...   * Image Open in GitHub Copilot app\n\n# prompt_async returns 204 after startup failure, leaving empty child sessions that appear frozen #33394\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nOpen\n\nOpen\n\nprompt_async returns 204 after startup failure, leaving empty child sessions that appear frozen#33394\n\nCopy link\n\nAssignees\n\n## Description\n\nDanRioDev\n\nopened on Jun 22, 2026\n\nIssue body actions\n\n## Summary\n\nDelegated/subagent sessions can be created successfully, then remain permanently empty while the caller/UI keeps polling them. The immediate trigger I observed was the known `session_message.seq` insertion bug, but the user-visible failure is broader: `POST /session/{id}/prompt_async` can fail during startup, publish/log `session.error`, still return HTTP 204, and leave a valid child session with zero user/assistant messages.\n\nThat makes delegation features look frozen: the child session exists, `/session/{id}/message` returns 200 with an empty list, and monitors/tools keep waiting for messages that will never be written.\n\nRelated existing reports/fix:\n\n  * BUG: session_message.seq NOT NULL constraint failed on agent-switched sessions #31204\n  * `opencode run` and HTTP `POST /session/{id}/message` fail with `NOT NULL constraint failed: session_message.seq` (1.15.13) #31413\n  * fix(core): guard against null event.seq when inserting session_messages #31419\n\nThose cover the `session_message.seq` crash itself. This issue is about the higher-level API/state behavior that turns a startup failure into a silent, empty, frozen child session.\n\n## Environment observed\n\n  * OpenCode version reported by CLI: `1.14.48`\n  * OS: macOS / darwin arm64\n  * Delegation plugin in use: `oh-my-openagent`\n  * Local DB: `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db`\n  * Local server logs: `~/.local/share/opencode/log/2026-06-22T143351.log`\n\n## What happened\n\nA parent session launched five delegated child sessions. Each child session row was created with a parent ID, title, directory, and model, but no message rows or parts were ever written.\n\nExample affected child sessions:\n    \n    `ses_1103540cfffe3v9MYa17MzZnc1  GDD Angle spine (@NeoAgent (Builder) subagent)\n    ses_110354087ffeubDsuGjBMTETWX  GDD social LLM (@NeoAgent (Builder) subagent)\n    ses_110354060ffe40fMS6hHJlilGS  GDD input feel rooms (@NeoAgent (Builder) subagent)\n    ses_110354044ffevRr4Bva6HfVaXQ  Engine Angle docs (@Odin (Builder) subagent)\n    ses_110353ffbffe1F9p3vKXuus3Ze  Angle spec capture (@Odin (Builder) subagent)\n    `\n\nDB state for those sessions:\n    \n    `session row: present\n    parent_id: present\n    message rows: 0\n    part rows: 0\n    session_message rows: 0\n    `\n\nThe logs show the important failure mode. For each child session, OpenCode logged `prompt_async failed [object Object]` and published `session.error`, but the HTTP request still returned 204:\n    \n    `POST /session/ses_1103540cfffe3v9MYa17MzZnc1/prompt_async\n    ERROR prompt_async failed [object Object]\n    service=bus type=session.error publishing\n    POST /session/ses_1103540cfffe3v9MYa17MzZnc1/prompt_async 204\n    `\n\nAfter that, the server/UI continued polling:\n    \n    `GET /session/ses_1103540cfffe3v9MYa17MzZnc1/message 200\n    GET /session/ses_110354087ffeubDsuGjBMTETWX/message 200\n    ...\n    `\n\nThose message responses were empty indefinitely because the initial user message was never persisted.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Bug] Plugin async prompts can overlap with Web prompt_async and create same-parent assistant siblings · Issue #28202 · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/28202)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The Assistant message schema declares `parentID: MessageID` as a single required reference (`packages/opencode/src/session/message-v2.ts:460`).\n\n# [Bug] Plugin async prompts can overlap with Web prompt_async and create same-parent assistant siblings #28202\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nClosed\n\n#28488\n\nClosed\n\n[Bug] Plugin async prompts can overlap with Web prompt_async and create same-parent assistant siblings#28202\n\n#28488\n\nCopy link\n\nAssignees\n\n## Description\n\nririnto\n\nopened on May 18, 2026\n\nIssue body actions\n\n### Description\n\nIn `opencode web` on `v1.15.4`, I observed a session where a real Web prompt and overlapping async prompt traffic produced multiple terminal assistant siblings under a single user message.\n\nThis was not only a visual duplicate in the Web UI. The persisted session data showed one real user message with four terminal assistant messages sharing that same user message as `parentID`. Each assistant message had `finish: \"stop\"`, and there was no tool-call continuation explaining the fan-out.\n\nI also observed a more severe variant (described below) where the trigger does not appear to require any plugin involvement: a plain Web user input racing with an in-flight tool continuation reproduces the same-`parentID` sibling pattern.\n\nThis looks like a session/turn-boundary invariant issue: overlapping prompt traffic on the same session should not create multiple independent terminal assistant siblings under one user message, and an in-flight tool continuation should not silently rebind to a newer user message.\n\n### Not a \"team mode\" artifact\n\nTo pre-empt the natural question: this is not legitimate team-mode fan-out.\n\n  * OpenCode core has no multi-agent fan-out under a single user message. The Assistant message schema declares `parentID: MessageID` as a single required reference (`packages/opencode/src/session/message-v2.ts:460`).\n  * The prompt loop creates exactly one assistant message per `lastUser` it observes (`packages/opencode/src/session/prompt.ts:1248-1305`).\n  * \"Team mode\" is a plugin-side concept; in the core, subagents are dispatched via the `task` tool which routes through the same single-loop pipeline rather than as same-parent siblings.\n\nMultiple terminal `finish: \"stop\"` assistant messages under a single user message is therefore not a design outcome of any core feature.\n\n### Severest observed variant\n\nA more severe variant I observed (with the plugin still installed, but with a trigger that does not appear to require it):\n\n  1. Start a session in `opencode web`.\n  2. Send user message U1 that triggers a tool call.\n  3. While the tool round-trip is in flight, send a second user message U2 from the same Web UI, timed to coincide with the tool output being delivered back to the model.\n  4. Observe that two terminal assistant messages are created, both with `parentID = U2` (not U1). There is no tool-call continuation between them, and U1's tool call appears to be effectively orphaned — its tool result is not followed by a model response under U1's branch.\n\nThe plugin was present, so I cannot fully rule it out as a contributing factor, but the trigger is plain Web user input racing with an in-flight tool continuation — a path that does not on inspection require plugin involvement. A clean reproduction without the plugin would confirm this, and I have not yet performed one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nopencode/packages/app/src/context/server-session.ts at dev · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/dev/packages/app/src/context/server-session.ts)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; if (message.type === \"assistant\" && touched.has(message.id) && parentID) touched.add(parentID) ... const applyV2 = (event: OpenCodeEvent) => {\n\n),\n\n),\n\n]\n\nfor (const parentID of parentIDs) {\n\nif (generations.get(sessionID) !== active) break\n\nconst parent = await fetchMessage(sessionID, parentID, () =>\n\nresetMessageLoad(sessionID, load, messageLoadBaseline(load, parentID)),\n\n).catch((error) => {\n\nconst cause = error instanceof Error && typeof error.cause === \"object\" ? error.cause : undefined\n\nif (cause && \"status\" in cause && cause.status === 404) {\n\nload.removedMessages.add(parentID)\n\nreturn\n\n}\n\nthrow error\n\n})\n\nif (!parent) continue\n\nif (parent.message.role !== \"user\") throw new Error(`Assistant parent is not a user message: ${parentID}`)\n\nparents.push(parent)\n\n}\n\n}\n\nif (generations.get(sessionID) !== active) return\n\nconst result =\n\nmode === \"prepend\"\n\n? page\n\n: {\n\n...page,\n\nsession: merge(\n\npage.session,\n\nparents.map((parent) => parent.message),\n\n),\n\npart: merge(\n\npage.part,\n\nparents.map((parent) => ({ id: parent.message.id, part: parent.parts })),\n\n),\n\n}\n\nconst preserveUnfetched =\n\nmode === \"prepend\" || (!result.complete && (!first || ((message: Message) => cmpMessage(message, first) < 0)))\n\napplyMessagePage(\n\nsessionID,\n\nresult,\n\nmessageLoads.get(sessionID) === load ? load : undefined,\n\npreserveUnfetched,\n\nmode !== \"prepend\",\n\n)\n\napplied = true\n\n} finally {\n\nif (!applied && generations.get(sessionID) === active && messageLoads.get(sessionID) === load) {\n\nconst touched = new Set(reduction.touched)\n\nlet parentID: string | undefined\n\nfor (const message of reduction.messages) {\n\nif (message.type === \"user\" || (message.type === \"synthetic\" && message.description?.trim()))\n\nparentID = message.id\n\nif (message.type === \"shell\") {\n\nif (touched.has(message.id)) touched.add(`${message.id}:assistant`)\n\nparentID = undefined\n\n}\n\nif (message.type === \"assistant\" && touched.has(message.id) && parentID) touched.add(parentID)\n\nif (message.type === \"compaction\" && touched.has(message.id) && parentID) touched.add(parentID)\n\n}\n\nconst normalized = normalizeSessionMessages(reduction.sessionID, reduction.messages)\n\nbatch(() => {\n\nfor (const message of normalized.messages) {\n\nif (!touched.has(message.id)) continue\n\napply({ type: \"message.updated\", properties: { sessionID: reduction.sessionID, info: message } })\n\n}\n\nfor (const messageID of touched) {\n\nconst next = normalized.parts.get(messageID) ?? []\n\nconst nextIDs = new Set(next.map((part) => part.id))\n\nfor (const part of next) {\n\napply({ type: \"message.part.updated\", properties: { sessionID: reduction.sessionID, part } })\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeb can persist duplicate final answers when client message IDs sort after assistant IDs · Issue #29478 · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/29478)\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; OpenCode Web can persist duplicate assistant final answers for one Web-originated user prompt on `1.15.10`. ... OpenChamber Web client appears to generate and submit the user `messageID` to `/session/:id/prompt_async`, so clock/order differences between client-side user IDs and server-side assistant IDs can trip this condition. ...     + lastAssistant.parentID === lastUser.id\nDirect CLI/TUI-style `opencode run --pure --agent build`  | no duplicate persisted; one assistant final answer\nCLI `opencode run --attach http://127.0.0.1:4096 --agent coordinator`  | no duplicate persisted; one assistant final answer\nWeb/OpenChamber session export  | duplicates persisted in backend/session JSON\n\n### Suspected root cause\n\nThe upstream source appears to still contain this loop exit condition in `packages/opencode/src/session/prompt.ts`:\n    \n    if (\n      lastAssistant?.finish &&\n      ![\"tool-calls\"].includes(lastAssistant.finish) &&\n      !hasToolCalls &&\n      lastUser.id < lastAssistant.id\n    ) {\n      break\n    }\n\nThat condition is unsafe when Web/client-generated user IDs and server-generated assistant IDs are compared lexicographically.\n\nOpenChamber Web client appears to generate and submit the user `messageID` to `/session/:id/prompt_async`, so clock/order differences between client-side user IDs and server-side assistant IDs can trip this condition.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nUse the assistant/user relationship instead of lexicographic ID order:\n    \n    if (\n      lastAssistant?.finish &&\n      ![\"tool-calls\"].includes(lastAssistant.finish) &&\n      !hasToolCalls &&\n    - lastUser.id < lastAssistant.id\n    + lastAssistant.parentID === lastUser.id\n    ) {\n      break\n    }\n\nRationale: the assistant message already records the user parent it answers. That relationship is the invariant the loop needs; message ID ordering is not reliable across client/server generation paths.\n\n### Impact\n\n  * Extra model call after a completed final answer\n  * Token/cache usage increase\n  * Persisted duplicate assistant messages pollute session context\n  * Display-only filtering would not fix the backend/session duplication\n\n\n### OpenCode version\n\n1.15.10\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nNo specific steps other than some messages return twice (new or existing session)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGET /session/:id/message returns 400 when messages contain format with inline JSON schema · Issue #26929 · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/26929)\nciteturn0search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; When sending a message via POST /session/:id/prompt_async with a format field: ... None (using opencode serve as HTTP API server with MCP servers configured via opencode.json)\n\n# GET /session/:id/message returns 400 when messages contain format with inline JSON schema #26929\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nOpen\n\n#37541\n\nOpen\n\nGET /session/:id/message returns 400 when messages contain format with inline JSON schema#26929\n\n\n## Description\n\ndhshah13\n\nopened on May 11, 2026\n\nIssue body actions\n\n### Description\n\nWhat happened?\n\nWhen sending a message via POST /session/:id/prompt_async with a format field:\n    \n    `{\n      \"format\": {\n        \"type\": \"json_schema\",\n        \"schema\": {\n          \"type\": \"object\",\n          \"properties\": {\n            \"status\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"created\", \"needs_info\"] },\n            \"summary\": { \"type\": \"string\" }\n          },\n          \"required\": [\"status\", \"summary\"]\n        },\n        \"retryCount\": 2\n      }\n    }\n    \n    `\n\nThe prompt is accepted (204), the LLM runs and completes successfully. But when calling GET /session/:id/message to retrieve messages, the server returns 400:\n    \n    `{\"name\":\"BadRequest\",\"data\":{\"message\":\"Expected OutputFormatJsonSchema, got {\\\"type\\\":\\\"json_schema\\\",\\\"schema\\\":{...}}\"}}\n    \n    `\n\nThe format field matches the OutputFormatJsonSchema definition in the OpenAPI spec at GET /doc exactly (type, schema, retryCount with additionalProperties: false). The message listing endpoint's deserialization rejects what the prompt endpoint accepted.\n\nThis appears to be a regression in the May 10-11 2026 releases that tightened HTTP API validation. The same workflow worked on versions before May 10.\n\n### Plugins\n\nNone (using opencode serve as HTTP API server with MCP servers configured via opencode.json)\n\n### OpenCode version\n\n1.14.48\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nSteps to reproduce:\n\n  1. Start opencode serve --port 4096\n  2. Create a session: POST /session with {\"title\": \"test\"}\n  3. Send a prompt with format: POST /session/:id/prompt_async with body containing \"format\": {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"name\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"name\"]}, \"retryCount\": 2}\n  4. Wait for the LLM to complete (poll GET /session/status until not busy)\n  5. List messages: GET /session/:id/message\n  6. Observe 400 Bad Request with \"Expected OutputFormatJsonSchema\" error\n\n### Screenshot and/or share link\n\nNo response\n\n### Operating System\n\nLinux (OpenShift container), also reproducible on macOS 25.4.0\n\n### Terminal\n\nN/A (HTTP API usage via Python httpx client)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nrunLoop fails to exit when client-generated messageID has clock skew ahead of server, causing infinite continuation with <system-reminder> wrap · Issue #28618 · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/28618)\nciteturn0search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last week; Root cause: The runLoop exit condition in `packages/opencode/src/session/prompt.ts:1268-1276` uses lexicographic comparison of message IDs (`lastUser.id < lastAssistant.id`) to decide whether the user message predates the assistant message. ... The webui passes a client-generated `messageID` to `session.promptAsync` (`packages/app/src/components/prompt-input/submit.ts:155-162`), so the user message's ID is timestamped with the browser's clock while the assistant message is timestamped with the server's clock.\nMessage IDs encode `Date.now()` in their first 12 hex chars (`packages/opencode/src/id/id.ts:51-69`). The webui passes a client-generated `messageID` to `session.promptAsync` (`packages/app/src/components/prompt-input/submit.ts:155-162`), so the user message's ID is timestamped with the browser's clock while the assistant message is timestamped with the server's clock. If the browser is even a few hundred ms ahead of the server, `lastUser.id` sorts after `lastAssistant.id`, the exit condition fails, the loop enters iteration 2, `step++` makes `step > 1`, and `prompt.ts:1400-1416` wraps the user message in `<system-reminder>The user sent the following message: …</system-reminder>` and fires another API request as if a fresh user message had just arrived.\n\nEvidence from a reproduction (opencode 1.15.5, c5y/claude-opus-4-7-fast, web client on host machine, server in Docker on another host):\n\nMessage  | ID  | Timestamp encoded in ID  | DB `time_created`  | Note\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nuser  | `msg_e4980c734001BQGJCPTvfloq9Y`  | 1779349636916  | 1779349634300  | client-generated, +2616ms ahead\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFeature Request: experimental.session.pre-compact Hook for Plugin-Controlled Context Transition · Issue #24958 · anomalyco/opencode · GitHub (https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/24958)\nciteturn0search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago;                 parentID: MessageID,       // Compaction trigger message ...      * OpenCode creates assistant message: `mode: \"pre-compact\"` (NOT `summary`)\n\n## Proposed Solution\n\n### Hook API\n    \n    {\n        \"experimental.session.pre-compact\": async (input, output) => {\n            // INPUT:\n            {\n                sessionID: string,         // Current session\n                auto: boolean,             // true=auto-compact, false=user /compact\n                parentID: MessageID,       // Compaction trigger message\n                messages: MessageV2[],     // Current conversation history\n            }\n            \n            // OUTPUT:\n            {\n                shouldRun: boolean,        // default: false — whether to run pre-compact\n                prompt?: string,           // Custom prompt for pre-compact round\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n### Behavior\n\n  1. Default (`shouldRun: false`): Zero impact. Compact proceeds normally.\n  2. When `shouldRun: true`:\n     * OpenCode creates assistant message: `mode: \"pre-compact\"` (NOT `summary`)\n     * AI receives prompt and full tool access\n     * AI executes tools (write, edit, read, etc.)\n     * After completion, OpenCode proceeds to normal compact\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n`opencode run` and HTTP `POST /session/{id}/message` fail wit